/**
 * Day 96 — Phase 5 country pack 6, South Asia: Nepal (ROADMAP item 6, fourth country).
 *
 * Before this, Nepal before 1815 was drawn only by empires based elsewhere: the schematic
 * `india-north` rings of the Maurya, Kushan, Gupta, Delhi, Mughal and British empires and the
 * Maratha `north-india-fringe` reached up over the hills to the Tibetan border, and the Tibetan
 * Empire's `tibet` ring reached down over the hills to the Terai (630–840). Nothing drew the
 * Licchavis, the Thakuris, the Malla valley, Simraungadh, the Khasa kingdom, the Baise and Chaubisi
 * hill kingdoms, the Sens or Gorkha. This pack adds 9 polities from c. 400 to 1813. The nations
 * layer draws Nepal (id `nepal`, from 1914); this pack uses other ids and has no keyframe after 1813.
 *
 * How it is built (docs/DATA.md, "Country-pack globe backfill"):
 *  - 18 new pieces from Natural Earth 1:10m admin-1 (public domain): Nepal's 14 zones, with the
 *    Terai cut off along the foot of the Siwaliks and split by longitude (the far west; Kapilvastu
 *    and Butwal; Parsa, Bara and Rautahat; Mithila; Morang), the hill zones grouped (the far west and
 *    Doti; Karnali; Bheri and Rapti; Dhaulagiri; Gandaki split into Kaski–Lamjung and Gorkha; Palpa;
 *    the Kathmandu valley with Nuwakot; Makwanpur and Chitwan; the eastern hills; Kirat), and
 *    Uttarakhand's hills, without the plains, split into Kumaon and Garhwal. Generator: et-tools
 *    day96-pieces.py (outside the repo).
 *  - A polity is a dated list of pieces; every change is a one-year step.
 *  - Every polity has a certainty level and sources, and links the Nepal timeline items.
 *
 * Not drawn (timeline items and pins only): the Kirata and Shakya periods and the Mauryan Terai
 * (left to the Maurya ring); Kirtipur, Patan and Bhaktapur as separate kingdoms after 1482 (one
 * valley shape); Khasa rule in western Tibet and Kumaon; the Chand kingdom of Kumaon and the
 * Garhwal kingdom before the Gorkha conquest; the Oiniwar kings of Mithila; Gorkha's hill states west
 * of Garhwal (1804–1814) and its occupation of western Sikkim (1789–1817); the western Terai (Awadh,
 * then the Company, until 1860).
 */
